{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling under docs/kernel.md sections 7 and 7.1: the Sequenced level.\n\nHugh placed the Sequenced level in the stable base on 10 November\n(order fc7576f8, item 3). Section 7.1 of docs/kernel.md holds it. The\nlevel adds a scope kind, a platform name, a level, six entry kinds and\na kind of reference between scopes. K1, K4, K5, K6 and K10 are open,\nand the level closes none of them. The rule offers no other path.\n\nI rule:\n1. The Sequenced level does not wait under section 7.1. The owner's\n   decision is the reason. The exception covers the first version\n   that the amended note cuts, and nothing else.\n2. The level is kernel under section 7. What an input may read when a\n   scope judges it is part of the judgment's meaning. Runtime and\n   replay must derive the same page from a cut, the same effects from\n   a prepare entry and the same effective fact from a commit entry. A\n   service outside the kernel can only deliver a member's state as\n   messages, and an application cannot read another scope.\n3. The level adds one to K1's count, the rules of platform:order@1,\n   and one kind to K4's closed list, order. Each head that builds the\n   level says so in its report.\n4. The amendment head for request ffbea8ed records this ruling in\n   docs/kernel.md, sections 7 and 7.1. The checker's review of that\n   head is the review that section 7 requires. If the checker refuses\n   the exception, the level waits and I return to Hugh.\n5. The Live level gets its own ruling with its design note.","body":{"to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}